  <section id="welcome" class="home-section" aria-labelledby="what-title">
    <h2 id="what-title">What does OCP do?</h2>
    <p>Keep one shared source for your laptop, desktop, and remote dev machine. OCP generates the configuration your coding-agent harness uses, with profiles for different kinds of work and overrides for each machine.</p>
    <p>It's useful if you work in something like <a href="https://t3.codes/">T3</a> and want the same agent instructions and skills wherever you run OpenCode. You can maintain the source yourself or <a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#agents">ask a coding agent to help</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="getting-started" class="home-section" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <h2 id="start-title">How do I get started?</h2>
    <p>You'll need <a href="https://opencode.ai/docs/">OpenCode installed and authenticated</a>. Start with a local source; you can add Git sync when you're ready to use another machine.</p>
    <ol class="start-steps">
      <li><h3>Install OCP</h3><p>On macOS or Linux, run:</p>{{ example('Install OCP', 'curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nandorocker/ocp/main/install.sh | sh') }}<p class="small">The installer checks the release checksum and puts OCP in <code>~/.local/bin</code>. See <a href="{{ '/install/' | url }}">installation instructions</a> for OS details and <code>PATH</code> help, or <a href="https://github.com/nandorocker/ocp/blob/main/install.sh">read the installer</a> first.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Set up your source</h3><p>Keep a backup of your existing OpenCode configuration before setup. OCP preserves it for <code>ocp reset</code>, then manages the global configuration path.</p>{{ example('Set up OCP', '~/.local/bin/ocp setup') }}<p>Follow the prompts to choose a new source or bring an existing setup. Your source is the directory of files you'll edit and keep. A profile is a named configuration for a kind of work, such as implementation or review.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Check the result and start a session</h3>{{ example('Start using OCP', '~/.local/bin/ocp status\nopencode') }}<p>Setup generates and activates a profile. Status shows which source and profile you're using; <code>opencode</code> starts the session. If you'd rather build your first profiles by hand, follow the <a href="{{ '/quick-start/' | url }}">quick start</a>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="source-title">
    <h2 id="source-title">One single source of truth for all your machines</h2>
    <p>Edit your source, then run <code>ocp apply</code> to generate the configuration. Profiles can inherit shared agents, skills, and settings without duplicating them. Use <code>ocp use &lt;profile&gt;</code> to switch profiles.</p>
    <p>When you put the source in Git, <code>ocp sync</code> brings changes between machines and renders your profiles. It can commit and push changes, so <a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#machines">read how sync works</a> before using it on a repository you share.</p>
    <p id="questions" class="closing">I'm <a href="https://nan.do">Nando Rossi</a>, the developer behind OCP. If you get stuck or have an idea, <a href="https://github.com/nandorocker/ocp/issues">open an issue</a>. I'd like to hear how it fits your setup.</p>
  </section>
